; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Pig Latin
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     words, recursion, MAP, Computer Science Logo Style
; SUMMARY:  Translates whatever you type into Pig Latin with the short recursive PIGL procedure made famous by Brian Harvey.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   You type a sentence and the program says it back in Pig Latin:
;   "hello world" becomes "ellohay orldway". Words that start with a
;   vowel just get "ay" on the end. Type quit (or press Enter on an
;   empty line) to stop.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and type sentences in the command center. You can also
;   call the pieces yourself:  print pigl "string  or
;   print piglatin [logo is fun]
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * FIRST, BUTFIRST and WORD to take words apart and glue them back
;   * a recursive operation that OUTPUTs its answer
;   * MAP to do the same thing to every word of a sentence
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make "y" count as a vowel too, and try "rhythm".
;   * Write UNPIGL, which turns Pig Latin back into English.
;   * Keep capital letters in the right place: "Sam" to "Amsay".
; ============================================================

; Brian Harvey's Computer Science Logo Style (volume 1) uses PIGL as one
; of its first examples of a recursive operation. It is only three
; lines long:
;     to pigl :word
;     if memberp first :word [a e i o u] [output word :word "ay]
;     output pigl word butfirst :word first :word
;     end
; Each time round, the first letter moves to the end, until a vowel is
; at the front. The book also points out the bug: a word with no vowel
; at all (like "sky") goes round and round forever. Our version counts
; how many letters it has moved, and gives up after a full turn.

to pigl :word
  output pigl.turn :word count :word
end

; LEFT is how many more letters we may still move before giving up.
to pigl.turn :word :left
  if :left = 0 [output word :word "ay]
  if vowelp first :word [output word :word "ay]
  output pigl.turn (word butfirst :word first :word) :left - 1
end

to vowelp :letter
  output memberp lowercase :letter [a e i o u]
end

; Numbers stay as they are: "7" in Pig Latin is still 7. A list typed
; inside brackets is left alone too.
to translate :word
  if or listp :word numberp :word [output :word]
  output pigl :word
end

to piglatin :sentence
  output map [translate ?] :sentence
end

; The conversation loop. READLIST gives us the line as a list of words.
; An empty answer, or the word quit, ends the program.
to chat
  print []
  print [Type a sentence, or quit to stop:]
  localmake "line readlist
  if emptyp :line [print [Ankthay ouyay! That means thank you.] stop]
  if memberp first :line [quit bye stop exit] [print [Oodbyegay!] stop]
  print piglatin :line
  chat
end

print [Welcome to the Pig Latin machine.]
print (sentence [For example:] piglatin [the turtle draws pictures])
chat
